DS="Ch 48 Disorders of sex development"; PR="Ch 56 Prostatitis, orchitis, epididymitis"; ER="Ch 68 Erection physiology and ED"; IN="Ch 81 Inguinal node dissection"; KU="Ch 84 Kidney and ureter anatomy"; MP="Ch 109 Male pelvic anatomy"; BP="Ch 145 BPH evaluation and medical therapy"
R=[
["Sex determination","Which statement about sex determination and differentiation is FALSE?",
 ["SRY is the same as the H-Y antigen","SRY lies on the short arm of Y","AMH is the first fetal testis hormone","DHT virilises the urogenital sinus"],0,
 "SRY (Yp, next to the pseudoautosomal boundary) is not H-Y antigen; its HMG domain bends DNA and it regulates SOX9, initiating testis formation. SF1 (NR5A1) is a nuclear receptor transcription factor regulating AMH and steroidogenic enzymes; mutations cause undervirilisation, adrenal insufficiency and gonadal dysgenesis. AMH (MIS) from Sertoli cells at 7–8 weeks is the first testicular hormone, acting locally/paracrine to regress Müllerian ducts; serum AMH marks testicular tissue in newborns. Testosterone enters cells by diffusion and acts locally on the Wolffian ducts (MIS and testosterone act unilaterally); in urogenital sinus, prostate and external genitalia 5α-reductase makes DHT. Normal ovarian development needs two X chromosomes; estrogens are not needed for female differentiation. Gender identity = self-identification as male or female; gender role = behaviour; conflicts underlie gender dysphoria. (Book outline lists chapters 49–52 next.)",DS,["c048-a01","c048-r01","c048-a02","c048-a03","c048-r18","c048-a05","c048-a06","c048-r02","c048-r12","c048-r11","c048-r03","c048-a23","c048-r21","c048-r22","c048-r23","c048-r24"]],
["Acute and chronic bacterial prostatitis","Which statement about bacterial prostatitis is FALSE?",
 ["Post-biopsy sepsis: give a fluoroquinolone","E. coli is the commonest organism","Abscess under 1–2 cm may need only antibiotics","Chronic bacterial: recurrent same-organism UTI"],0,
 "AUA: fever after biopsy should not get fluoroquinolones or TMP-SMX; resuscitate and give carbapenem, amikacin or 2nd/3rd-generation cephalosporin. Category I: acute dysuria, frequency, fever; E. coli commonest (Pseudomonas commoner after manipulation); organisms differ by cause (spontaneous, post-TURP, post-biopsy). Long drainage: suprapubic tube. No response: image for abscess (<1–2 cm antibiotics; larger: aspiration or transurethral unroofing). Chronic bacterial: recurrent UTI with the same organism, well between episodes; exclude retention; under 45 no imaging, check flow and residual for stricture (four-glass test no longer routine); prostate-penetrating antibiotics; refractory: daily suppression or TURP. Category IV: asymptomatic inflammation found on BPH, cancer or infertility work-up; no treatment (don't treat raised PSA with antibiotics; follow PSA and DRE). IgG4 disease: IgG4 plasma cells and fibrosis, steroid-responsive (also Ormond disease).",PR,["c056-a05","c056-r04","c056-a04","c056-r01","c056-r02","c056-r03","c056-r05","c056-r06","c056-a06","c056-r07","c056-r08","c056-r09","c056-a07","c056-r10","c056-r11","c056-r12","c056-a02","c056-a18","c056-a03"]],
["Penile anatomy and haemodynamics","Which statement about erectile anatomy is FALSE?",
 ["Ischiocavernosus relaxes in the rigid phase","Ventral tunica lacks the outer longitudinal layer","Tunica has types I and III collagen","About 15% rely on one pudendal source only"],0,
 "Rigid phase needs ischiocavernosus contraction to push blood in, on top of arterial dilation, sinusoidal relaxation, raised pressure and venous occlusion. The outer longitudinal tunica layer is absent at 5–7 o'clock, so prostheses erode ventrally toward the urethra. Tunica: types I and III collagen (no II), outer longitudinal, inner circular, middle oblique layer; erection needs no venous valves. ~70% have both internal and accessory pudendal supply; ~15% depend on one source (vulnerable at pelvic surgery); vascular anatomy varies. Erection needs arterial inflow plus venous coaptation and intact corporal structure and neural pathways (brain, cord, periphery).",ER,["c068-a04","c068-a01","c068-a02","c068-a03","c068-r01","c068-r02","c068-r03","c068-r04"]],
["Inguinal staging","Which statement about inguinal staging in penile cancer is FALSE?",
 ["Clear groins often hide pelvic nodes","17–25% of cN0 men harbour micrometastases","Expert DSNB false-negative rate is under 5%","Immediate ILND beats delayed salvage"],0,
 "Spread is stepwise (superficial then deep inguinal, then pelvic, then distant); pelvic metastases do not occur with negative inguinal nodes. Nodal metastasis is the main prognostic factor. 17–25% of clinically node-negative men have micrometastases; immediate ILND improves survival over salvage for clinically detectable disease. DSNB: >80% sensitivity, <5% false-negative in expert centres, long learning curve; positive → ILND. Superficial or modified ILND for impalpable nodes (less morbid); if superficial nodes are negative the deep package rarely is, though modified templates may miss lateral superficial nodes and frozen section misses >25%. No imaging is formally recommended for the cN0 groin; CT chest/abdomen/pelvis with nodal involvement or suspected pelvic nodes.",IN,["c081-r08","c081-a01","c081-r01","c081-a04","c081-r02","c081-a05","c081-r03","c081-r04","c081-a06","c081-a03"]],
["Renal anatomy","Which statement about renal anatomy is FALSE?",
 ["Renal veins are segmental end vessels","Hilum anterior to posterior: vein, artery, pelvis","Columns of Bertin hold interlobar arteries","Gerota fascia is open inferiorly"],0,
 "Five segmental arteries are end arteries (segmental infarction if occluded; segments resectable); veins have extensive collaterals (infundibular collars, subcapsular plexus), so segmental vein occlusion causes no harm. The posterior segmental artery passes behind the pelvis; if anterior to the ureter it can cause UPJO. Brödel line: variable avascular plane between anterior and posterior segments. Hilum: vein, artery, pelvis (anterior to posterior). Columns of Bertin: cortex between pyramids with interlobar arteries; cortex ~1 cm. Kidneys move ~3 cm with inspiration or standing; medial aspect rotated 30° anteriorly; 12th rib over right, 11th–12th over left. Gerota is closed superiorly and laterally (continuous with diaphragmatic fascia), open inferiorly. Transperitoneal access: mobilise colon along the line of Toldt. US: kidneys isoechoic to liver overall; cortex and pyramids hypoechoic; sinus hyperechoic (fat). CTA is the gold standard for renal vessels. Lumbar veins enter renal veins (commoner on left).",KU,["c084-a08","c084-r09","c084-a06","c084-r08","c084-a07","c084-r05","c084-r04","c084-a04","c084-r03","c084-a01","c084-r01","c084-r02","c084-a02","c084-r07","c084-a03","c084-a05","c084-a09","c084-r06"]],
["Pelvic wall and vessels","Which statement about male pelvic anatomy is FALSE?",
 ["Perineal extravasation tracks down the thigh","Accessory obturator veins occur in about 50%","Femoral nerve injury impairs knee extension","Direct hernias lie medial to inferior epigastrics"],0,
 "Scarpa fascia joins Colles fascia (continuous with dartos) and fuses to thigh fascia: extravasation is confined (butterfly perineal haematoma), never down the legs or into buttocks, but can track up to the clavicles. Accessory obturator veins (50%) drain to the underside of the external iliac vein; accessory obturator artery (25%) from the inferior epigastric runs medial to the femoral vein. Femoral nerve (L2–L4): anterior thigh sensation, knee extension; it runs in psoas, genitofemoral on psoas (retractor palsy; sutures perpendicular may entrap); obturator nerve: adductors. Sacrospinous ligament separates greater and lesser sciatic foramina. Superficial inferior epigastric vessels bleed at inguinal incisions and ports. Conjoint tendon (internal oblique + transversalis) reinforces the posterior inguinal wall; direct hernia medial, indirect lateral to inferior epigastric vessels. Superior vesical artery arises from the patent obliterated umbilical artery. Ureter crosses anterior to the common iliac artery; pelvic ureter supply enters laterally (incise peritoneum medially). Sympathetic input to the pelvic plexus via the superior hypogastric plexus and sacral sympathetic trunks; parasympathetic from sacral intermediolateral column. Infant bladder is intra-abdominal. Pudendal nerve first branch: dorsal nerve of penis (ventral in Alcock canal).",MP,["c109-a03","c109-r01","c109-a04","c109-r05","c109-a05","c109-r06","c109-r07","c109-a01","c109-a02","c109-r02","c109-r03","c109-r04","c109-r14","c109-a07","c109-r15","c109-a06","c109-r16","c109-a12"]],
["LUTS evaluation","Which statement about evaluating LUTS is FALSE?",
 ["Invasive urodynamics are routine at first visit","Frequency-volume charts should cover 72 hours","DRE estimates volume and screens for cancer","Raised creatinine warrants renal ultrasound"],0,
 "LUTS are multifactorial (enlargement, obstruction, neurological disease, bladder dysfunction); symptom severity is the first step. DRE at first visit. 3-day frequency-volume chart. Upper tract imaging if creatinine is raised; cytology for storage symptoms in smokers. PSA if a cancer diagnosis would change management (life expectancy >10 years). US and flow for moderate-severe symptoms. Invasive urodynamics diagnose obstruction and are considered when detrusor over- or underactivity is suspected. Detrusor overactivity in up to 83% with severe obstruction (may persist after treatment). Loop diuretics worsen symptoms.",BP,["c145-a08","c145-r04","c145-a04","c145-a03","c145-a05","c145-a06","c145-a07","c145-r02","c145-r01","c145-r03","c145-a01","c145-a12","c145-a02"]],
["Chromosomal and gonadal DSD","Which statement about chromosomal and gonadal DSD is FALSE?",
 ["Frasier syndrome predisposes to Wilms tumour","Klinefelter raises breast cancer risk about 8×","Y material in Turner raises gonadoblastoma risk","Mixed gonadal dysgenesis is second to CAH"],0,
 "WT1 mutations cause Denys-Drash (Wilms tumour, early-onset proteinuric nephropathy with hypertension and renal failure, DSD; nephropathy is the common denominator) and Frasier (gonadoblastoma, nephropathy, gonadal dysgenesis; no Wilms predisposition). Klinefelter (commonest 47,XXY): tall, eunuchoid, gynecomastia, small testes, azoospermia, poor muscle; breast cancer ~8×. Turner (45,X): short, webbed neck, cubitus valgus, infantilism, horseshoe kidney; Y material raises gonadoblastoma risk (~30%) in hypoplastic abdominal streak gonads. 46,XX complete gonadal dysgenesis: no Turner stigmata; 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis: up to 35% tumours by 30. Testicular regression/anorchia: 46,XY, castrate testosterone, high LH/FSH (detectable in mini-puberty 2–6 months), undetectable AMH. Mixed gonadal dysgenesis: second commonest DSD; unilateral testis, contralateral streak, Müllerian remnants, variable masculinisation. Ovotesticular DSD: ovarian follicles plus seminiferous tubules (one ovary and one testis or, commonly, ovotestes; unilateral or bilateral; testis part dysgenetic); fertility potential (as female) guides gender assignment.",DS,["c048-a04","c048-a10","c048-a07","c048-r17","c048-r04","c048-r05","c048-r06","c048-a08","c048-a09","c048-a11","c048-r13","c048-a12","c048-r19","c048-a13"]],
["CP/CPPS","Which statement about chronic prostatitis/chronic pelvic pain syndrome (CP/CPPS) is FALSE?",
 ["Inflammation degree tracks symptom severity","Catastrophising predicts pain and depression","IBS is the commonest overlapping condition","Antibiotic plus α-blocker gives best response"],0,
 "Inflammation in EPS, VB3 or semen does not correlate with symptoms (CPCRN: asymptomatic men had as much); bacteria are as common as in controls. No inflammation = IIIB. Central sensitisation (afferent and efferent), raised pain sensitivity, low ACTH with blunted stress response. Helplessness and catastrophising predict pain, urinary symptoms and depression. Commonest overlapping condition: IBS (also fibromyalgia, chronic fatigue). Flares predicted only by recent sex and UTI-like symptoms. More ejaculatory pain and premature ejaculation. GUPI adds bladder pain (~40% in MAPP). Diagnosis of exclusion; issues beyond the pelvis. Cystoscopy optional (low flow/high residual for stricture; bladder pain for Hunner lesion). Pseudodyssynergy: sphincter activity during voiding without straining (pelvic floor dysfunction). Treatment: multimodal by UPOINT (N = neurologic/systemic: neuropathic drugs); meta-analysis best for antibiotic + α-blocker; no repeated antibiotics without positive culture; pelvic floor tenderness: pelvic floor PT/myofascial release; bladder pain: bladder therapies; SNM if urgency and frequency.",PR,["c056-a08","c056-r15","c056-r13","c056-a01","c056-r14","c056-r18","c056-r17","c056-a09","c056-r16","c056-a10","c056-r21","c056-a11","c056-a12","c056-a13","c056-r19","c056-r20","c056-a14","c056-a15","c056-r23","c056-a19","c056-a16","c056-r25","c056-r24","c056-a17","c056-r22","c056-r27","c056-r26","c056-r28","c056-r29"]],
["Erection signalling","Which statement about erection signalling is FALSE?",
 ["Rho kinase promotes muscle relaxation","nNOS in nerve terminals starts erection","cGMP then activates protein kinase G","Hyperpolarisation favours erection"],0,
 "Rho kinase phosphorylates (inhibits) myosin light-chain phosphatase, sustaining contraction and opposing erection. Nerve terminal nNOS initiates erection; endothelial eNOS maintains it (the book's keyed option has these reversed). NO stimulates guanylate cyclase → cGMP → PKG, which opens K+ and closes Ca2+ channels; low cytosolic calcium and hyperpolarisation relax muscle (the key event); PDE degrades cGMP to restore tone. NOS converts arginine and O2 to citrulline and NO; three isoforms (nNOS, eNOS physiological; iNOS with stress). Carbon monoxide (haem oxygenase) aids erection. VIP has little effect; dopamine, oxytocin, serotonin act centrally; norepinephrine also involved. Low adenosine deaminase/sickle models show priapism (adenosine excess). Sonic hedgehog falls after cavernous nerve injury; reduces apoptosis in animals but unproven in men. Nerve injury worsens inflammation (M1 macrophages). Brain imaging: reduced temporal/parietal activity (introspection) disinhibits; default mode and salience networks and sympathetic tone affect psychogenic ED.",ER,["c068-a11","c068-a06","c068-r08","c068-r09","c068-r10","c068-r11","c068-r07","c068-r12","c068-a16","c068-a12","c068-a05","c068-r05","c068-a13","c068-a10","c068-a17","c068-a14","c068-a15","c068-r06"]],
["ILND technique and outcome","Which statement about inguinal lymphadenectomy is FALSE?",
 ["Sartorius transposition prevents complications","Camper fascia carries skin flap blood supply","Endoscopic ILND mainly reduces wound problems","1–3 positive nodes: 5-year survival 75–80%"],0,
 "Sartorius transposition aims to protect femoral vessels but has predicted complications in some series. Skin flaps survive on vessels in Camper fascia (lymphatics run beneath it, so it can stay with the skin). Endoscopic (video) ILND: oncologically equivalent node yield, less morbidity, mainly fewer wound complications; both legs at one sitting; lymphatic and bleeding complications depend on energy and care. Robotics not superior to laparoscopy; Xi allows repositioning without re-docking. Radical ILND for palpable disease (curative for inguinal-only; palliation of pain, infection, bleeding). Adjuvant chemotherapy helps node-positive men. 5-year survival: 1–3 positive nodes 75–80%; pelvic, bilateral or ENE <10%.",IN,["c081-a09","c081-a02","c081-a10","c081-a07","c081-r05","c081-a08","c081-r06","c081-r07","c081-a11","c081-a12"]],
["Ureteral anatomy","Which statement about ureteral anatomy is FALSE?",
 ["Distal ureter supply enters medially","Stripping adventitia risks stricture","Mid-ureter is supplied from behind","Lateral orifices tend to reflux more"],0,
 "Ureteral blood supply: medial proximally (renal artery, aorta), posterior in the mid-ureter (common iliac), lateral distally (superior vesical): incise pelvic peritoneum medial to the ureter. Removing periureteral adventitia or lateral retraction causes ischaemia and stricture. Trigone base is the interureteric ridge (Mercier bar). Orifice position A (medial trigone) to C (lateral wall): the more abnormal the configuration, the more lateral. Medial deviation of both pelvic ureters: RPF, pelvic lipomatosis, after abdominoperineal resection; one ureter: nodes, diverticulum, hypogastric aneurysm (may be normal in women).",KU,["c084-a12","c084-r11","c084-a11","c084-r10","c084-a13","c084-a14","c084-a10"]],
["Bladder, prostate and urethra","Which statement about bladder and prostate anatomy is FALSE?",
 ["Antireflux closure is sphincteric","Ligate bladder vessels near the bladder","Striated sphincter lies just distal to verumontanum","Female bladder neck has little sympathetic input"],0,
 "The intravesical ureter is a passive flap valve (pliant submucosal ureter backed by detrusor), not a sphincter. Trigone: ureteric muscle forms a sheet; interureteric crest (Mercier bar) and Bell muscle; three layers (superficial ureteric longitudinal, deep from Waldeyer sheath to bladder neck, detrusor). Bladder supply: vesical branches or any internal iliac branch; ligate near the bladder to spare pelvic plexus nerves; lateral pedicle lateral and posterior pedicle posteromedial to the ureter. Bladder lymph: mainly external iliac, also obturator, internal and common iliac: include all in cystectomy. Male bladder neck richly sympathetic; female sparse. Prostate arteries enter at 4 and 8 o'clock; lymph to obturator/internal iliac, also presacral/external iliac; Denonvilliers fascia separates prostate from rectum; cavernous nerves pass seminal vesicle tips, within lateral endopelvic fascia, most vulnerable at the apex. Membranous urethra 2–2.5 cm (1.2–5); striated sphincter is signet-ring shaped (not a flat sheet), just distal to the verumontanum: deep resection there causes incontinence. Penile lymphatics cross to both groins; scrotal ones don't cross the raphe; penis, scrotum and perineum drain to inguinal nodes. Fournier: spermatic fasciae are embryologically distinct and often spared (book). Pelvic MRI with contrast (T1 enhancement, T2 anatomy) best shows tumour invasion.",MP,["c109-a08","c109-a09","c109-r17","c109-a10","c109-a16","c109-r18","c109-a15","c109-r08","c109-r09","c109-r10","c109-r11","c109-r13","c109-a11","c109-a18","c109-r12","c109-a13","c109-a14","c109-a17"]],
["Conservative care and AUR","Which statement about watchful waiting and acute retention is FALSE?",
 ["Recurrent AUR responds well to medication","Mild LUTS without risk: watchful waiting","TWOC succeeds in about 60% after first AUR","α-Blockers improve TWOC success"],0,
 "Recurrent AUR is an indication for surgery. Watchful waiting (mild symptoms, low progression risk): education, reassurance, lifestyle (fluid timing; limit tea, caffeine, alcohol; review medicines), yearly follow-up. AUR is a common complication; catheterise then TWOC (~60% succeed), with α-blockers to improve success; 5-ARIs reduce AUR risk.",BP,["c145-a11","c145-a09","c145-r05","c145-a10","c145-r06","c145-r07","c145-r08","c145-r15","c145-r16","c145-a40","c145-r17","c145-r18"]],
["CAH, androgen insensitivity, PMDS","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["A palpable gonad suggests CAH","21-Hydroxylase causes 95% of CAH","Complete AIS identifies as female","5α-reductase deficiency virilises at puberty"],0,
 "A palpable gonad (testis, rarely ovotestis) effectively rules out virilised female CAH. CAH: 21-hydroxylase (CYP21A2 conversion to pseudogene in 65–90% classic) causes 95%; 75% salt-wasting, 25% simple virilising; diagnose by 17-OH-progesterone at 3–4 days; prenatal dexamethasone (before 9 weeks) is effective though started before diagnosis; bilateral adrenalectomy for severe uncontrollable cases. Testosterone biosynthesis defects (first three enzymes in adrenal and testis) can impair glucocorticoid/mineralocorticoid synthesis. Complete AIS: 46,XY, testes, female genitalia, no Müllerian structures, raised female; female identity (rare male cases); tumours after puberty in 1–2%. Partial AIS: reduced receptor number or binding (AR at Xq12). 5α-reductase type 2 deficiency: high T/DHT ratio; partial masculinisation at puberty but no prostate growth. PMDS (AMH or receptor defect): 46,XY, normal male genitalia, uterus and tubes; transverse testicular ectopia in 30–50%. MRKH: absent uterus/vagina, primary amenorrhoea; atypical form (10%) has more urinary tract anomalies. Hypospadias with cryptorchidism: DSD 30% overall, 15% if palpable, 50% if impalpable; karyotype for bilateral impalpable testes or unilateral impalpable with hypospadias.",DS,["c048-r16","c048-r09","c048-a14","c048-r07","c048-a15","c048-r08","c048-a16","c048-a17","c048-r20","c048-r14","c048-a18","c048-a19","c048-a20","c048-r15","c048-a21","c048-a22","c048-r10"]],
["Epididymitis and orchialgia","Which statement about scrotal inflammation and pain is FALSE?",
 ["Enteric organisms cause most epididymitis under 35","Mumps is the commonest viral orchitis","Autoimmune orchitis features antisperm antibodies","Cord denervation targets the trifecta nerve complex"],0,
 "Under 35, STIs (especially Chlamydia, even if sexual history is denied) cause most epididymitis; enteric organisms over 35. Mumps is the commonest viral orchitis. Autoimmune orchitis: antisperm antibodies; secondary forms with vasculitis (Behçet, PAN, HSP). Scrotal pain: duplex Doppler for torsion, mass or anomaly. Pelvic floor PT for orchialgia with abnormal tone (central pain features); exhaust conservative care before surgery. Microsurgical cord denervation: cremasteric fibres, perivasal sheath, posterior lipomatous tissue (trifecta; most Wallerian degeneration). Post-vasectomy pain epididymectomy: include the distal vasectomy site. Orchiectomy for chronic pain: inguinal approach does better.",PR,["c056-r33","c056-a21","c056-r30","c056-a20","c056-r31","c056-r32","c056-a22","c056-a23","c056-r34","c056-a24","c056-r35","c056-a25"]],
["Causes of ED","Which statement about the causes of ED is FALSE?",
 ["ACE inhibitors commonly cause ED","Thiazides and β-blockers are linked to ED","ESRD raises prolactin, contributing to ED","ED can be a sentinel of vascular disease"],0,
 "ACE inhibitors have no known adverse effect and may help; thiazides and β-blockers are linked to ED; commonest drug classes: antiandrogens, antidepressants, antihypertensives. ESRD: uraemia, vascular change, raised (not low) prolactin; ED may persist after transplant. Ageing affects central, sensory, hormonal, neural, vascular and penile mechanisms. Diabetes and metabolic syndrome give severe ED. ED is a sentinel for chronic disease. Primary lifelong ED: psychogenic, congenital arterial insufficiency, penile anomalies, vascular defects. Testosterone therapy only with low levels (<~300 ng/dL) AND compatible symptoms.",ER,["c068-a08","c068-r17","c068-a09","c068-r16","c068-r13","c068-r15","c068-r14","c068-r18","c068-a07"]],
["α-Blockers","Which statement about α-blockers for LUTS is FALSE?",
 ["β1 receptors are key α-blocker targets","Tamsulosin is uroselective (α1A/α1D)","Silodosin lowers BOOI most","Naftopidil targets α1D receptors"],0,
 "β1 receptors are scarce in the LUT and not targeted. Uroselective agents (tamsulosin, silodosin, alfuzosin) bind α1A/α1D and are best tolerated; naftopidil is α1D-selective. First line for moderate-severe LUTS. Terazosin cut IPSS 22% (first phase II trial); terazosin/doxazosin need titration (first-dose hypotension); dizziness from α1B vascular and central effects; doxazosin postural hypotension RR 2.7. Tamsulosin relieves symptoms within a week (flow within hours); tamsulosin 0.4 mg equals silodosin 8 mg; silodosin gives the largest BOOI fall (−30.4). Warn about first-dose hypotension with any agent. IFIS cannot be prevented: tell the ophthalmologist. MTOPS: doxazosin reduced progression versus placebo but less than finasteride or combination.",BP,["c145-a13","c145-a14","c145-r10","c145-a21","c145-a22","c145-r09","c145-a15","c145-a19","c145-a16","c145-a25","c145-a17","c145-a20","c145-a23","c145-a24","c145-a26","c145-a18"]],
["Hypospadias with undescended testis","A newborn has proximal hypospadias and an impalpable left testis. The risk of a DSD is about:",
 ["50%","5%","15%","30%"],0,
 "With hypospadias and cryptorchidism, DSD risk is 30% overall, 15% if the testis is palpable, and 50% if impalpable: karyotype and full DSD evaluation.",DS,["c048-a22","c048-r10"]],
["5-ARIs, combinations and others","Which statement about other BPH drugs is FALSE?",
 ["PDE5 inhibitors improve flow rates","5-ARIs shrink the prostate 15–30%","CombAT: dutasteride cut surgery vs tamsulosin","Antimuscarinic trials excluded PVR >200 mL"],0,
 "PDE5 inhibitors relax prostate and bladder neck smooth muscle (NO) and lower IPSS by 2–4 points versus placebo, but do not improve flow; alone or with α-blockers. 5-ARIs shrink the prostate 15–30% over 6–24 months, reduce progression, AUR and surgery; warn of depressive symptoms; PSA changes within a month (volume and flow need longer). Book Q27 claims finasteride is a 'type 1' 5-ARI that doesn't lower serum DHT: incorrect; finasteride inhibits type 2 and lowers serum DHT by about 70% (dutasteride both types, ~90%). MTOPS: doxazosin + finasteride most reduced progression. CombAT: dutasteride lowered surgery risk more than tamsulosin. Large gland with bothersome voiding symptoms: α-blocker + 5-ARI. Storage symptoms: add antimuscarinic (trials excluded PVR >200 mL) or mirabegron (better tolerated in the elderly). Phytotherapy: mechanisms uncertain; Serenoa meta-analysis showed no benefit (heterogeneous extracts); hexane extract and Serenoa + lycopene + selenium encouraging.",BP,["c145-a33","c145-a32","c145-a34","c145-r13","c145-a28","c145-r11","c145-a29","c145-a27","c145-a35","c145-a36","c145-a37","c145-r12","c145-a30","c145-a31","c145-a38","c145-a39","c145-r14"]],
]
